    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d372">

                <pb facs="0435" n="419" sameAs="frus:frus1947v08/pg419"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.24/12–3047</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Key</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Chief of the Division of Brazilian
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dawson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-12-30">December 30,
                        1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Allan</hi>: I have just received your letter of
                    December 19, 1947<anchor n="70" xml:id="d372-f1"/> and we are all greatly
                    relieved to learn that the question of <pb facs="0436" n="420" xml:id="pg420"/>
                    offering the lend-leased war vessels to Brazil has been shelved for the
                    present.</p>
                <p>I was also interested to learn that the “nominal” price of $10,000 has been
                    placed on each of the German vessels which were turned over to the Brazilians.
                    This is probably $9,999 more than the Brazilians expect to pay but maybe I am
                    wrong about this and in any event the value of these ships probably considerably
                    exceeds the price you mention. I shall, of course, keep the figure under my hat
                    for the present.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">David</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
